@page step_106-Intro

<h1>Results</h1>

<h2>Flow around a 2D cylinder</h2>

When you run the program on one or more processors, you should get an output similar to this:
@code
Running on 8 MPI rank(s)...
Number of degrees of freedom: 44907
Checking no-slip enforcement on cylinder:
||uh||_L2   = 1.77254
||uh||_Linf = 1.00005

Time step 1 : time = 0.05
Newton iter.  0:  nonlinear residual = 4.19804040e-01
Newton iter.  1:  nonlinear residual = 1.62384047e-01
Newton iter.  2:  nonlinear residual = 2.46070592e-03
Newton iter.  3:  nonlinear residual = 2.95946157e-06
Newton iter.  4:  nonlinear residual = 3.26622040e-12
Stopping because residual is below prescribed tolerance (1.00e-10)
Checking no-slip enforcement on cylinder:
||uh||_L2   = 6.16e-20
||uh||_Linf = 1.88e-19

Time step 2 : time = 1.00e-01
Newton iter.  0:  nonlinear residual = 3.06157361e+00
Newton iter.  1:  nonlinear residual = 1.09536818e-03
Newton iter.  2:  nonlinear residual = 4.07793944e-07
Newton iter.  3:  nonlinear residual = 7.90742372e-14
Stopping because residual is below prescribed tolerance (1.00e-10)
Checking no-slip enforcement on cylinder:
||uh||_L2   = 9.60e-21
||uh||_Linf = 2.83e-20

Time step 3 : time = 1.50e-01
Newton iter.  0:  nonlinear residual = 8.05773329e-02
Newton iter.  1:  nonlinear residual = 5.46615098e-04
Newton iter.  2:  nonlinear residual = 9.87374839e-08
Newton iter.  3:  nonlinear residual = 1.56679516e-14
Stopping because residual is below prescribed tolerance (1.00e-10)
Checking no-slip enforcement on cylinder:
||uh||_L2   = 1.76e-21
||uh||_Linf = 5.73e-21


... and so on until time integration finishes.

@endcode

In particular, the significant numbers of the Newton residual should be the same
as this output, regardless of the number of processes used to run the computation.
Notice that the no-slip constraint is not satisfied for the initial condition,
since at that point no computation has been run yet, and the Lagrange multiplier
have not yet been set to satisfy the constraint.
Aside from the first time step for which Newton's method needs
to find a Lagrange multiplier coming from a zero candidate,
almost all subsequent time steps converge in 3 nonlinear iterations,
which is optimal (the 0-th iteration shows the norm of the RHS,
but does not solve any linear system).

To limit the run time and the number of outputs, the example program only runs
for 5 seconds. If we run it for \f$t_\text{end} = 200\f$ seconds instead, we see that the Von Karman streets
instability starts appearing at around \f$t = 30\f$.
The time the instabilitiy triggers
typically depends on a few factors, such as the spatial and temporal accuracy,
and the symmetry (or lack thereof) in the mesh and initial and boundary conditions.
The velocity magnitude at \f$t = 10\f$ and 100 looks like this:

<table width="100%">
<tr>
<td>
  <img src="../figures/t10.svg" width="100%" alt="Velocity magnitude at t = 10s">
</td>
<td>
  <img src="../figures/t100.svg" width="100%" alt="Pressure solution">
</td>
</tr>
</table>

This is, at least visually, not different from the flow around a cylinder obtained by
applying a strong no-slip condition, which is reassuring, especially
since the no-slip is enforced exactly in this case.

From the force file written at each time step, we can postprocess the nondimensional quantities
which are typically presented for this benchmark, that is, the force (drag and lift) coefficients,
and the Strouhal number, which is the nondimensional vortex shedding frequency:

@f{equation}
C_D = \frac{F_x}{\frac{1}{2}\rho U_\infty D}, ~~~~~ C_L = \frac{F_y}{\frac{1}{2}\rho U_\infty D}
~~~~~ St = \frac{fD}{U}.
@f}

The two signals for \f$t \in [0, 200]\f$ look like the figure below,
and we can compare their mean values and amplitudes after reaching the periodic
regime with the values available in the literature.

<img src="../figures/forces.png" width="50%" alt="Velocity magnitude at t = 10s">

From the last 100 seconds of simulation, we obtain the indicators in the table below.
The results are compared with those of Mendes et al. (see the table for the reference),
and with the same benchmark of the Lethe project, a CFD-DEM solver based on deal.II.
The vortex shedding frequency \f$f\f$ is obtained through a fast Fourier transform on the periodic part of the signal.
The obtained values (especially the amplitudes) differ from the ones from the literature,
likely because our simulation is neither converged in time nor in space,
and would benefit from a finer mesh (before and around the cylinder, and in the wake),
and either a finer time step or a more accurate
time stepping method, such as a BDF2 scheme.
Nevertheless, the obtained signals and velocity field qualitatively match the expected
results for this problem, and the vortex-shedding frequency is on par with the available results.

