<h2>Flow around a 3D cylinder</h2>

@note Running the 3D example requires substantially more memory than in 2D.
By default, MUMPS allocates a fixed-size memory pool for its internal use, and this example
likely exceeds it, which causes MUMPS to fail with an error code -9.
However, the <code>SparseDirectMUMPS</code> solver currently does not check the return code
from MUMPS, which then returns a solution with NaNs.
To bypass this, you can run the solver while asking MUMPS to extend this memory pool by 100%,
which can be done by passing the PETSc runtime flag <code>-mat_mumps_icntl_14 100</code>.
Your call would then look like:
<code>mpirun -n <np> step-106 -mat_mumps_icntl_14 100</code>.

TODO: quite slow.

<a name="step-106-extensions"></a>
<h2>Possibilities for extensions</h2>

<h3>Move to an iterative solver</h3>
The most obvious improvement to this program would be to use an efficient preconditioner,
to move away from the direct solver, which greatly limits the 3D capabilities of the current example.
Possible solutions could involve a block Schur preconditioner for the monolithic system,
or a geometric multigrid method.

<h3>Define the Lagrange multiplier only on the boundary</h3>
The second obvious improvement is to get rid of all the unused Lagrange multiplier dofs,
which are costly in memory and solve time. As mentioned in the introduction, a quite elegant way
of achieving this while still manipulating a single <code>DoFHandler</code> is to leverage
the \f$hp\f$-machinery, and define different <code>FESystem</code>s on the mesh cells, depending
on whether they touch the cylinder or not. On cells that do not touch the cylinder, we can set
the Lagrange multiplier space to a <code>FENothing</code>, which does not add any additional dof.
Although this still adds unused volume dofs in cells adjacent to the cylinder, this greatly reduces
the overall number of unused degrees of freedom.

*/